---
name: commitment_memory
description: >
  The commitment a finished conversation produced, as a first-person PLAN memory
  in the participant's own stream (backend/cognition.py,
  apply_conversation_outcome; issue #778). Written when the #582 outcome pass
  reports `plans_changed: true` with a non-blank `commitment`. Before this, the
  commitment reached only `maybe_revise_plan` -- and the default plan_mode
  "schedule" wires MockPlanner, whose revise() returns the plan unchanged -- so
  an agreement the model stated outright was dropped and re-negotiated on every
  cooldown expiry. The other person is named in full deliberately, matching
  relationship_memory: the conversation path retrieves with
  `query=listener.name`, so naming them is what makes the prior surface in the
  dialogue that needs it. Memory text, not a live model prompt.
inputs:
  other:
    type: string
    description: The person the commitment was made with, e.g. "Chris Donnelly".
  commitment:
    type: string
    description: >
      The commitment, verbatim from the `conversation_outcome` tool call -- the
      agent's own words for what it agreed to, so it is never rewritten. Only a
      real, non-blank commitment is written; the transcript is deliberately NOT
      used as a fallback, since a whole transcript stored as a "plan" is noise.
sample:
  other: Chris Donnelly
  commitment: Pizza place near campus with Chris Donnelly in about 20 minutes
---
I agreed with {{ other }}: {{ commitment }}
